What is the difference between Auto Commit and Manual Commit in Kafka?

1. First, understand Offset Commit
When a Kafka consumer reads messages, it needs to tell Kafka how far it has progressed.

This process is called Offset Commit.

There are two approaches.
2. Auto Commit vs Manual Commit
Feature - 	Auto Commit	- Manual Commit
Offset management - 	Kafka client commits automatically	- Application commits explicitly
Control	- Less control	- More control
Configuration	- enable.auto.commit=true	- enable.auto.commit=false
Risk	- May commit before business processing finishes - 	Can commit after successful processing
Use case	- Simpler processing scenarios	- Critical business processing

Kafka supports automatic and manual offset commits.

In auto commit, Kafka commits offsets periodically without requiring explicit application intervention.

In manual commit, the application controls when offsets are committed. We can commit after successful message processing.

For critical business operations, I generally prefer manual offset management because it gives better control over message processing and helps avoid message loss due to premature commits.

However, manual commits alone do not guarantee exactly-once business processing. We should also consider idempotency and duplicate handling."
